 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c016", "issues": [
   {"issue": "\"Material Defect\" is defined partly by non-conformity \"to the review criteria specified in this Section\", criteria the Section never separately states, and the layered 90-day, 360-day and second-anniversary periods leave it unclear whether notice and cure are conditions precedent to any repurchase claim. Both the trigger and the timing of the sole remedy are open to opposing readings.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.50},
   {"issue": "This Section's sole-remedy sentence runs to \"the Indenture Trustee, the Issuer, the Depositor and any Noteholder\", while Section 2.02(b) states the same limitation for the Issuing Entity, Noteholders, Certificateholders, Indenture Trustee and Owner Trustee and Section 2.03(b) for only the Issuing Entity, Noteholders and Indenture Trustee, with the Note Insurer omitted throughout. Who is bound by the sole remedy cannot be settled by harmonising the three sentences.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.44}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c017", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The opening words condition the subsection on a purchase made \"in accordance with Subsections 2.02(a) or (b) above\", but subsection (b) is this very subsection, so the cross-reference is circular. The intended trigger (probably Sections 2.02(a) and 2.03) has to be supplied by construction.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.40},
   {"issue": "The sole-remedy sentence here names the Issuing Entity, Noteholders, Certificateholders, Indenture Trustee and Owner Trustee, a list that differs from the parallel sentences in Section 2.02(a) and Section 2.03(b) and again omits the Note Insurer. The three provisions cannot be reconciled on the scope of the remedy limitation.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.36}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c018", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The bracketed parenthetical reserving to the Sponsor the right to enforce breaches \"with respect to the HELOCs of the Servicer under the Servicing Agreement\" is garbled and opened with a brace rather than a parenthesis, so which party retains which enforcement rights is unclear. The same sentence purports to assign both \"rights and obligations\", which sits oddly with the closing sentence of Section 2.08 that no obligations are assigned.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.35},
   {"issue": "This sole-remedy sentence binds the Issuing Entity, Indenture Trustee, Noteholders and Certificateholders, whereas the corresponding sentence in Section 2.03(b) omits Certificateholders and the Note Insurer. The instrument therefore states the same limitation with materially different beneficiaries.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.33}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c019", "issues": [
   {"issue": "The repurchase obligation turns on a breach that \"materially and adversely affects the value of the interests\" and on \"discovery or receipt of notice\", so whether prompt written notice and the 90-day cure window are conditions precedent to a claim, and by what measure materiality is judged, are both open. These are the words on which a repurchase dispute would turn.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.55},
   {"issue": "The concluding sole-remedy sentence runs only to \"the Issuing Entity, the Noteholders or the Indenture Trustee\", omitting the Certificateholders and Note Insurer who are expressly covered by the parallel sentences in Sections 2.02(b) and 2.03(a). Whether the Note Insurer's remedies are limited cannot be resolved by reading the three together.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.44}
 ]},
 {"clause_id": "c020", "issues": [
